You cannot modify the `selectorText` property directly in Firefox, IE, or Edge because, as you mentioned, it is read-only. However, you can work around this limitation by creating a new CSS rule with the desired selector and copying the properties from the original rule to the new one.

Here's how you can do it:

```javascript
// Original rule
var originalRule = document.styleSheets[0].cssRules[0];

// Create a new rule with the desired selector
var newRule = document.createElement('style');
newRule.type = 'text/css';
newRule.styleSheet.cssText = ' .another-class { ' + originalRule.style.cssText + ' }';

// Append the new rule to the head of the document
document.head.appendChild(newRule);

// Now you can modify the properties of the new rule
newRule.style.color = '#123456'; // Change the color, for example

// If you want to remove the rule later, just use:
document.head.removeChild(newRule);
```

