Yes, you can iterate through the elements of the ith bucket in an std::unordered_multiset. Here's how you can do it:

```cpp
#include <unordered_multiset>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::unordered_multiset<int> hashtable;
    hashtable.insert(1);
    hashtable.insert(2);
    hashtable.insert(3);
    hashtable.insert(2);

    // Let's say bucket i is the one containing the value 2
    int bucketIndex = hashtable.bucket_count() - 1; // Get the index of the last bucket

    for (auto& element : hashtable[bucketIndex]) {
        std::cout << element << " ";
    }
    return 0;
}
```

In this example, `bucket_count()` returns the total number of buckets, and `-1` is used to get the index of the last bucket (since bucket indices start from 0). The `for` loop iterates over each element in the specified bucket, printing it to the console.

