Solution: Unfortunately, no. The @section directive in MVC Razor requires a symbol (not a string) as its first argument. This is by design, as it allows the compiler to generate a unique name for each section, which helps prevent naming conflicts and ensures that sections are properly scoped. While it would be convenient to use a string constant for the section name, doing so would compromise the benefits of this design choice. Therefore, you cannot use a string constant directly as the name of a section in MVC Razor. However, you can achieve a similar effect by defining a constant symbol in a class and using that symbol as the section name. Here's how you can do it:

```csharp
public class MyConstants
{
    public const string SomeSectionName = "SomeString";
}
```

Then, in your view, you can reference this constant like this:

```csharp
@section MyConstants.SomeSectionName

@RenderSection(MyConstants.SomeSectionName, required:false)
```

This approach maintains the benefits of using unique, scoped section names while allowing you to define them using string constants.

