samples.general_completion:
Here is a method that converts a uint to a char[] without generating garbage:

```csharp
public static char[] ToCharArray(uint value)
{
    const int maxLength = 10;

    char[] result = new char[maxLength];

    if (value == 0)
    {
        Array.Copy(new char[] { '0' }, result, 1, 1);
        return result;
    }

    int startIndex = maxLength - 1;
    int index = startIndex;
    do
    {
        result[index] = (char)('0' + value % 10);
        value /= 10;
        --index;
    }
    while (value != 0);

    int length = startIndex - index;

    Array.Copy(result, 0, result, index + 1, length);

    return result;
}
```

This method creates a new char[] array, but it does not generate garbage because it does not create any temporary objects. The new array is directly assigned to the result variable, which is a value type (a struct), and hence does not create a new object on the heap.

The method first checks if the value is 0, and if so, it copies a single '0' character into the result array.

