One way to avoid duplicating code with multiple constructors is to use factory methods or builder patterns. Factory methods allow you to define an abstract factory that generates instances of a class, while builder patterns allow you to construct complex objects step by step. Here's how you can implement these solutions:

Factory Method:
1. Create an interface or abstract class with a method that returns an instance of the class.
2. Implement this method in a concrete class to return an instance of the desired subclass.

Example in Java:

```java
interface AnimalFactory {
    Animal createAnimal(String name);
}

class Dog implements Animal {
    // Dog implementation
}

class Cat implements Animal {
    // Cat implementation
}

class AnimalFactoryImpl implements AnimalFactory {
    public Animal createAnimal(String name) {
        if ("Dog".equals(name)) {
            return new Dog();
        } else if ("Cat".equals(name)) {
            return new Cat();
        } else {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown animal type: " + name);
        }
